{:edb/db-before-t 2472 :edb/db-after-t 2473 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535785 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:10:14.340Z" 13194139535785 true] [17592186220677 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220679 13194139535785 true] [17592186220681 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220684 13194139535785 true] [17592186221387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366193 13194139535785 true] [17592186221387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366199 13194139535785 true] [17592186221388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366205 13194139535785 true] [17592186221388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366214 13194139535785 true] [17592186221950 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535785 true] [17592186221950 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139535785 true] [17592186221950 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = e^{x} + x^{2} + y, y(0) = 1$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$y(\\frac{1}{2}) \\approx$ {{field-1}},\n$y(1) \\approx$ {{field-2}}" 13194139535785 false] [17592186221950 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+{e}^{1/2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈3.898\\,721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y≈3.898\\,721.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈2(3.898\\,721)+{e}^{1}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ≈11.515\\,724\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, rounded to $2$ decimal places, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.90,\\,y(1)≈11.52.\n$$" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366194 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1089 "q-339693" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy+y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1113 #uuid "32118057-b660-5919-beb6-d21b2129e10b" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366193 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈-0.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.67.$" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366195 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366196 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366197 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366198 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1041 "field-1" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366198 13194139535785 true] [17592186366194 1110 #uuid "493d66e0-1ff0-5516-916f-224a16122077" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366195 1034 "-3.55" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535785 true] [17592186366195 1111 #uuid "fc753ea7-a417-56c1-99b8-5ea5adb32c79" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366196 1034 "-0.32" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535785 true] [17592186366196 1111 #uuid "b7eb3536-4b06-599a-8207-8aea091cca69" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366197 1034 "0.32" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535785 true] [17592186366197 1111 #uuid "a0b1595f-6f6c-5a49-a166-ffd91506ea34" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366198 1034 "-0.67" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535785 true] [17592186366198 1111 #uuid "bef51703-4788-5d65-b4f0-daf828fb5737" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366200 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1089 "q-339694" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(3x-2),\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(3)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1113 #uuid "02054168-89d5-599b-a2c5-093e7487797c" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366199 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{(1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2))} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5-6\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.31,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-0.31.$" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366201 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366202 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366203 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366204 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1041 "field-1" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366204 13194139535785 true] [17592186366200 1110 #uuid "471ff64a-598d-577d-8084-10f3399448ee" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366201 1034 "-1.52" 13194139535785 true] [17592186366201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1